  "answer": "The use of toxic materials, such as pesticides, herbicides, and fungicides, can have harmful effects on an ecosystem. These chemicals are often indiscriminate in their action, meaning they can harm not only the target pests but also other organisms, including predators and prey, which can disrupt the balance of the ecosystem. When these toxic materials get mixed up in the soil, they can cause even more dangerous effects. Some pesticides are degradable and break down into harmless substances in a short time, while others are non-degradable and contain harmful elements like mercury, arsenic, or lead. These non-degradable pesticides can accumulate in the bodies of animals and pass through the food web. This process of entry of pollutants into a food chain is known as bioaccumulation. As these pollutants move from one trophic level to the next, they tend to concentrate, which is known as biomagnification. This means that animals at the top of the food chain, such as large predators, may receive enough of the pollutant to cause significant harm.",
